  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 312 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nAnother upper-level system approaches the region beginning on \nMonday, providing an active weather pattern through at least the \nmiddle part of next week. Rain chances start to increase for the \nwestern half of the region as the storm system approaches. Long-\nrange guidance has what is going to be the remnants of Hurricane \nPolo being absorbed into the aforementioned storm system. This is \ngoing to supply vast amounts of upper-level and surface moisture \ntransport, resulting in areawide rain chances by Monday night \nlasting through Wednesday. Guidance has indicated Tuesday will be \nthe \"rainiest\" day per the positioning of both systems. Ensemble \nguidance also suggests PWATs reaching near and even exceeding \ndaily maximum climatological values throughout Tuesday. Therefore,\nthe threat of heavy rainfall leading to flash flooding returns. \nThere remains uncertainty regarding the exact setup and specific \nimpacts with this upcoming system. We will continue to monitor \ntrends closely. Besides the rain, a cold front is forecast to move\nthrough the region sometime on Wednesday/Wednesday night bringing\ncooler temperatures in the upper 70s to mid 80s. Drier conditions\nand similar temperatures (low to upper 80s) are expected behind \nthe front Thursday and Friday. \n\nLamberson",
